# iru_custom_app (Resource)

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

Configure an existing `file_key`, or set `source_file` and
`source_sha256 = filesha256(...)` to use the documented
[Iru upload workflow](https://api-docs.iru.com/). Local bytes are hashed before
requesting an upload ticket and while streaming to S3. The Iru bearer token is
never sent to S3, and signed upload credentials are not stored in state.
Metadata-only updates retain the existing installer; changing the configured
checksum uploads new bytes and replaces its reference in place.

Creation omits absent optional script/location fields. Updates retain a nonblank
name and omit unchanged optional fields rather than resending blank defaults.
Changed empty script values still request clearing, and installer replacement
still sends its explicit new file key.

The public API documents no detached-upload deletion operation. Destroy deletes
the Library Item and retains any underlying upload artifacts. A failed or
ambiguous upload can leave an orphan; no automatic replay or cleanup is attempted.
Reported `sha256` and `file_size` remain null when omitted by the API. A changed
remote installer hash is drift. If Iru omits the hash but changes the file key,
a managed local source is conservatively uploaded again rather than assuming
that its bytes still match.

After a verified local-source upload, the provider waits five seconds before
the first Library Item write, matching Iru's
[irupkg upload flow](https://github.com/kandji-inc/irupkg/blob/3fe1ec778c9e9fe7e7f7308dc8785c90e431df68/src/irupkg/helpers/utils.py).
This grace period does not prove processing readiness. Custom Apps have no
documented upload-status endpoint in the reviewed public API; the separate IPA
status endpoint must not be guessed for Custom Apps. The grace period is locally
tested and has not yet been validated against a live Custom App upload.

Create/update are never automatically replayed, including HTTP 503 while an
installer may be processing. After an ambiguous creation failure, inspect Iru
and import any existing Library Item before retrying. A valid returned UUID is
retained when later response validation fails.
Terraform marks a creation error tainted. Verify the persisted item and its full
configuration before deliberately untainting or removing/re-importing its state;
do not accept a replacement plan blindly.

New items default to inactive, no restart and no Self Service. Continuous
enforcement requires an audit script; `no_enforcement` requires Self Service and
an existing category UUID. Hidden category clearing is not assumed. Scripts are
sensitive in displays but remain in state. Destroy permanently deletes the
Library Item and can affect external assignments. Consider Terraform
`prevent_destroy` where appropriate. Real tenant compatibility remains unverified.
Iru can strip outer script whitespace. Known configured spelling is retained for
equivalent responses; substantive changes remain drift. Imported scripts should
match the returned content for the first no-op plan.

